RISC-V: INT arguments and results are sign-extended by the side that produces them. Applied after perf-48; JIT only.
- The RV64 psABI has the caller sign-extend a 32-bit integer argument and the callee its 32-bit integer
  result, but the JIT did neither: genPutArgReg and genSimpleReturn skipped the move, and with it the
  sext.w, whenever the value was already in the ABI register. So every callee that took an INT parameter
  or call result to be sign-extended (perf-43) re-extended it in place: a sext.w in the prolog per such
  parameter, and one after the call per such result.
- genPutArgReg and genSimpleReturn now sign-extend an INT in place unless Lowering::IsInt32SignExtendedInReg
  already knows it is. In return, an INT parameter is taken to be extended as it arrives, and
  IsInt32SignExtendedInReg accepts the result of an INT call (IsInt32ResultSignExtended): compiled code
  extends it by the rule above and native code by the psABI. Helper calls and struct returns, whose
  register holds a struct's bytes, are not relied on; a local mapped from a struct parameter's register
  keeps its prolog sext.w.
- Native callees gain the extension the psABI asks of their callers; native callers already provide it.

